*Fundamental of pods:--

*When a pod gets created, it is scheduled to run on a node in your cluster.

*The pod remains on that node until the process is terminated, the pod object is deleted , the ppod is evicted
for lock of resources or the node foils.

*if a pod is scheduled to a node that fails or , if the scheduling operation itself fails , the pod is 
 deleted.

*A given pod (UID) is not "rescheduled" to a new node, instead it will be replaces by an identical pod
with even the same name if desired, but with a new UID.

*Volume in a POD will exists as long as that POD (with that UID) exist if that  POD is deleted for any reason, volume is also destroyed and created as new on new pod.

* A controller can create and manage multiple pods handling replication, rollout and providing self-healing capacities
